@nsobid-4F5C96E2D97F45AC288BC67613EBEF0A

# Xenoblade Chronicles 2 3.0.1 - WIP Dynamic 60 FPS Cutscenes

@flag print_values
@flag offset_shift 0x100

// Runs gameplay, menus and real-time cutscenes at up to 60 FPS with a step
// that follows the real frame time.
//
// The game paces itself with a CPU wait of (1/60 s) x swap interval, and its
// clock hands every subsystem the step stored in the framework object. Once
// per presented frame, right before the present call, the patch sets the swap
// interval to 1 and the step to the measured frame time, clamped to 1/60 to
// 1/30 s so the game keeps real-time speed down to 30 FPS. The step x 30 also
// drives a 30 Hz tick: an accumulator gains the step x 30 each frame, and the
// tick fires on the update that carries it over a whole frame, then drops
// that frame. The layer (UI) manager gets the step, and the wind simulation
// reads it in place of its own 1/30 constant. The clock's scaled step keeps
// its speed factor, which pauses and slows the game, and a speed change keeps
// the current step instead of resetting it to the config rate. The previous
// counter value, the tick and its accumulator are kept in unused memory past
// the last .bss variable, next to the step copies below.
// Menu waits counted in updates still count updates and last longer below
// 60 FPS.
//
// Events (cutscenes) run on a timeline counted in whole 30 Hz frames. In
// sound-synced events the timeline follows the voice and BGM position
// (seconds x 30) and holds whenever it would get more than one frame ahead,
// so above 30 updates per second it steps or holds by itself and stays in
// time with the audio. Event components depend on those whole-frame steps.
// Events that are not audio-locked have no hold and would play fast, so they
// now step only on updates where the 30 Hz tick fires and hold the timeline
// on the others, the way a pause does, stepping whole frames at 30 per second
// like the audio-locked ones. The first step from frame 0 always goes
// through: the title screen's event gets one update before the title pauses
// it, and holding that update left the camera at the map's origin instead of
// the sky. Event objects also run their own 30 Hz frame timers, the delays
// before event voices and BGM start among them. The float timers now count
// down by the step x 30, and the integer waits start at their frame count x
// updates per frame (the frame count x 4096 / the fixed-point step x 30,
// rounded). One load wait whose action fires partway down its count keeps
// both values doubled, which is exact at 60 FPS.
//
// Characters would still move in those 30 Hz steps. Both character event
// components get an even per-update step instead: a tracking loop moves them
// by the step x 30 each update, plus an eighth of the gap between a filtered
// lag and half a frame. The lag (timeline frame minus tracked time) jumps by a
// whole frame on every timeline step, so it goes through a one-pole filter
// (a quarter of the change per update) first, and the per-update step stays
// within about 1% of even. Jumps and seeks pass through unchanged. The event
// camera gets the same step: its tracks advance by the timeline delta and only
// ran on updates where the timeline stepped, so at 60 FPS the camera moved in
// 30 Hz steps while the characters moved every update, and the characters
// shook back and forth against the camera. Its component now smooths the step
// first like the character components. Event cameras are baked per frame and
// sampled between frames, so a cut baked into the data (a jump between two
// consecutive frames) would be drawn halfway through the jump over two or
// three updates, which stock never shows. When the jump from the current
// frame to the next is more than 4x both neighbouring jumps and over 0.35
// units, the camera samples the current frame, as stock does, and switches to
// the frame after the cut on the update where the timeline itself reaches
// it, which is when the characters move to the next shot. The baked
// rotation is a set of Euler angles in degrees blended per channel, and the
// data can switch to an equivalent set (x - 180, 180 - y, z - 180) or wrap at
// 180 between two frames. Stock only shows whole frames, but a blend across
// either swings the camera, so the next frame's angles are first brought into
// the current frame's form. The character command lists also run on hold
// updates. A command's call counter only marks its first call, and each
// pending start delay gains one on those updates so it still counts 30 Hz
// frames. Commands that turn the step into a playback rate (action speed,
// move and attachment animation) take a full-frame rate from an unused slot
// in the character's step copy: the tracked per-update step over the step x
// 30, which is 1 at a steady pace at any frame rate and drops while the
// timeline waits on the audio, as the stock step does. A fixed 1 there kept
// characters moving through those waits, so they drifted ahead of the
// cutscene. The attachment animation divides the per-update step by the step
// x 30 the same way, so animations keep their authored speed. The
// loop's state word sits in padding after the event manager's step block, the
// filter state past the last .bss variable, and its code in dead repeated
// static-init blocks.
//
// Character skeletal animation stores bone translation and scale as cubic
// segments fitted through the per-frame values, exact on whole frames. Where a
// cutscene cuts inside a segment (a bone moving several units at once), the
// cubic swings far past both ends between those frames, and the character's
// limbs fly through the next shot for a few updates. A segment whose jump is
// over 4x both neighbouring segments' and over 0.35 units per frame is sampled
// on the whole frame, as stock does.
//
// Effects that follow a path, such as the diving helmet lamps, sample baked
// per-frame transform tracks that advance with their command list on timeline
// frames only, so they moved in 30 Hz steps against characters that move every
// update. The list stays on whole frames, since its commands start and end
// effects and lights by frame. On the updates after the list runs, each
// running track is sampled again with a zero step at its shown frame plus the
// characters' smoothed steps since, which moves the effect on the characters'
// time without advancing its clock or firing its keyed events.
//
// Particle effects run on a fixed-step accumulator that gains 1.0 per update
// and simulates one effect frame each time it reaches 1.0. It now gains, tests
// and steps by the game step x 30, so effects keep their authored speed at any
// step. Slow-motion group rates already scale this step below 1.0 at stock.
//
// A few gameplay waits count updates in 30 Hz frames: battle AI script frame
// counts, the script frame(N) wait, the player camera's input lock after a
// reset, and the hold and exit wait at the end of a battle sequence. Each
// update they now advance by the game step x 30, which the frame code stores
// with a 12-bit fixed-point copy in unused memory past the last .bss variable.
// This is exact at any step.
//
// Menu layouts animate on the layer step, and menu fades convert their frame
// counts to seconds, so both follow the step. Menu scripts also wait a number
// of updates, which would halve at 60 FPS. Most menus share one wait step that
// copies the next queued count into a {bool, int} countdown; its start block
// now stores the count doubled and returns through the step's own return path,
// in the same six instructions. The remaining countdowns start from constants
// or seconds x 30 and are doubled where they are set. The compass keeps a
// two-update icon hold: its start value shares a register with a state enum,
// and the hold is 66 ms at stock.
//
// Menu layout animations are baked with one key per animation frame, and the
// layer applies the key at the whole frame with no blending, so menus and
// pop-ups still animate at 30 Hz. The keyframe sampler now blends the current
// key with the next one by the fractional frame when the next key is exactly
// one frame later and the clock's whole frame is the current key's frame,
// which leaves holds and jumps as authored. The position, matrix and colour
// channels take both key values and blend them, and the pattern channel keeps
// the current key. The setup code fits in the sampler's parent combine block,
// which is rewritten more compactly, and in the room the blending channels
// leave free.
//
// Everything left on 30 Hz units converts authored frame counts to seconds
// (x 1/30) and does not depend on the update rate. Pre-rendered movies play
// through a separate player and are unaffected. Below 30 FPS the game slows
// down. The frame code and its step helpers sit in dead repeated static-init
// blocks, whose guards are already set when the code runs.
@enabled
// Frame rate: up to 60 FPS, and a 30 Hz tick for the event timeline
